Narrow every LFG proposal member's role to exactly one role bit before it goes
on the wire, so no server path can throw Lua errors in a player's client.
Touches: src/server/game/Handlers/LFGHandler.cpp, WorldSession::SendLfgUpdateProposal()

Why: the 3.3.5 client resolves a proposal member's role mask down to a single
role icon and hard-errors on anything it cannot resolve --

  Interface\FrameXML\LFGFrame.lua:337: Unknown role: UNKNOWN
  ... GetTexCoordsForRole -> LFDDungeonReadyStatus_UpdateIcon
      -> LFDDungeonReadyPopup_Update

-- thrown repeatedly while the dungeon-ready pop-up is up and again on accept.
Both an empty mask and a mask that still has several role bits set land there.

Patch 0003 fixed one producer of empty masks (bots dropped into a group by
FillLfgRaidGroup, outside the queue, never get an LFG role). It did not fix the
error, because more than one path reaches this packet with a mask the client
cannot render:

  * LFGQueue::CheckCompatibility only requires CheckGroupRoles() to succeed on
    the non-raid multi-entry path. The single-entry path ignores the result, and
    the raid path ignores it too -- and CheckGroupRoles cannot succeed for a
    raid, since its quotas are the 5-man ones (1 tank, 1 healer, 3 dps). Any
    check whose queue entries mention a raid dungeon takes that branch, and a
    bot's LfgJoinAction happily puts raid dungeons in its selection alongside
    normal ones. Whenever the result is ignored, every member keeps the mask
    they queued with: someone who ticked Tank and DPS stays TANK|DAMAGE.
  * The same function forms proposals with an incomplete group once bot fill is
    allowed (OnLfgAllowBotFill, after AiPlayerbot.LfgDungeonBotFillSec), which
    is what puts those un-narrowed masks in front of a real client in the first
    place.

Rather than chase each producer, narrow at the one place the value reaches the
wire. The role bits collapse to tank > healer > damage, with damage as the
fallback for a roleless member.

2026-08-23: the leader bit is now stripped as well. It was originally preserved
on the assumption that the client reads it separately for a crown, which was
never checked -- and a report of the same Lua error came in hours after this
narrowing shipped. The running binary provably had the patch (the log string is
in it) and the correction log below had fired zero times over the whole uptime,
so every mask reaching that client already carried exactly one role bit. The
leader bit was the only thing this function still let through untouched, and
LFGQueue.cpp copies the queue mask verbatim into the proposal
(`data.role = itRoles->second`), so a solo queuer -- their own leader -- has
their own entry go out as LEADER|DAMAGE = 9. Nothing in the proposal pop-up
draws a crown, so there is no reason to send the bit. A corrected mask is logged (at error level either way -- the "lfg" logger is
commented out in worldserver.conf, so it falls back to Logger.root, which only
passes error and above) so the remaining producer names itself in the log next
time rather than being guessed at.

Server-side role bookkeeping is untouched: MakeNewGroup still hands the raw mask
to Group::AddMember/SetLfgRoles exactly as before.

--- a/src/server/game/Handlers/LFGHandler.cpp
+++ b/src/server/game/Handlers/LFGHandler.cpp
@@ WorldSession::SendLfgUpdateProposal
+static uint8 NarrowProposalRoleForClient(uint8 role)
+{
+    uint8 const leader = role & lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_LEADER;
+
+    if (role & lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_TANK)
+        return leader | lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_TANK;
+
+    if (role & lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_HEALER)
+        return leader | lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_HEALER;
+
+    // Covers both "already damage" and "no role at all": damage is the one role
+    // every class can fill, and roleless members sort last either way.
+    return leader | lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_DAMAGE;
+}
+
 void WorldSession::SendLfgUpdateProposal(lfg::LfgProposal const& proposal)
 {
@@ (sort by roles: tank, healer, dps)
     for (auto const& [pguid, player] : proposal.players)
     {
-        if (player.role & lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_TANK)
-            tanks.push_back(pguid);
-        else if (player.role & lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_HEALER)
-            healers.push_back(pguid);
-        else
-            dps.push_back(pguid);
+        // Sort on the narrowed role so the order matches the icons actually sent
+        switch (NarrowProposalRoleForClient(player.role) & ~lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_LEADER)
+        {
+            case lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_TANK:
+                tanks.push_back(pguid);
+                break;
+            case lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_HEALER:
+                healers.push_back(pguid);
+                break;
+            default:
+                dps.push_back(pguid);
+                break;
+        }
     }
@@ (per-player block)
         lfg::LfgProposalPlayer const& player = proposal.players.find(pguid)->second;
-        data << uint32(player.role);                       // Role
+
+        uint8 const role = NarrowProposalRoleForClient(player.role);
+        if (role != player.role)
+        {
+            if (!(player.role & (lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_TANK | lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_HEALER | lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_DAMAGE)))
+                LOG_ERROR("lfg", "SMSG_LFG_PROPOSAL_UPDATE: proposal {} member [{}] has no LFG role at all (mask {}), sending damage - a roleless member throws Lua errors client-side",
+                    proposal.id, pguid.ToString(), uint32(player.role));
+            else
+                // Error rather than warn on purpose: the unconfigured "lfg" logger
+                // falls back to Logger.root, which only passes error and above
+                LOG_ERROR("lfg", "SMSG_LFG_PROPOSAL_UPDATE: proposal {} member [{}] role mask {} was never narrowed to one role, sending {}",
+                    proposal.id, pguid.ToString(), uint32(player.role), uint32(role));
+        }
+
+        data << uint32(role);                              // Role
         data << uint8(pguid == guid);                      // Self player
